---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { site } from '../../data/site';

const title = 'About SensForge — Who We Are & Why These Tools Exist';
const description =
  'SensForge is an independent, player-run toolset for competitive FPS aiming: who builds it, how the calculators are tested, and how the site stays free.';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', href: '/' },
  { name: 'About' },
];
---

<Base title={title} description={description} breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs} canonical="/about/">
  <div class="wrap-narrow">
    <Breadcrumbs items={breadcrumbs} />

    <h1>About SensForge</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      We build the aiming calculators we kept wishing existed while switching between competitive
      shooters ourselves — precise about the maths, honest about the limits, free of clutter.
    </p>

    <section class="section">
      <h2 id="why">Why this site exists</h2>
      <p>
        Converting your aim between games should not require forum archaeology or signing up for an app.
        The maths is public: each game rotates the camera by a documented constant, and preserving physical
        mouse distance (cm/360) is all it takes to keep your muscle memory. SensForge packages that maths
        into fast, no-nonsense tools that run entirely in your browser.
      </p>
      <p>
        We also noticed a lot of tools in this niche quietly disagree with each other about game constants,
        with no way to tell which values are verified and which were copied from another site. So every
        value we ship carries a source label and confidence level on our <a href="/methodology/">methodology
        page</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2 id="how">How the tools are built &amp; tested</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Formulas from first principles.</strong> The converters use the yaw model documented by game developers and cross-checked against independent measurement projects; see the <a href="/methodology/">methodology page</a> for each constant's provenance.</li>
        <li><strong>Sanity-tested against hand calculations.</strong> Every calculator's example tables are recomputed from the same formulas the tool runs, so the numbers you see are reproducible.</li>
        <li><strong>Client-side only.</strong> No build step sends your inputs anywhere — there is no server-side component that could even receive them.</li>
        <li><strong>Corrections are welcome and credited.</strong> If a constant or formula is wrong, <a href="/contact/">tell us</a> and we will fix it with a changelog entry.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2 id="money">How the site makes money</h2>
      <p>
        Right now: nothing. SensForge is independent and self-funded. If we ever add optional
        sponsorships or affiliate links, they will be clearly labelled (<code>rel="sponsored"</code>) and
        will never affect formulas, rankings or recommendations. Tools stay free.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2 id="people">Who runs it</h2>
      <p>
        SensForge is run by a small team of competitive FPS players and software engineers. We play the
        games we build for — currently heavy Valorant, CS2 and an unreasonable amount of aim-trainer time.
      </p>
      <p class="small muted">
        For press, corrections or partnership enquiries: <a href="/contact/">contact page</a> or
        {site.email}.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
